  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how electromagnetic interactions in solids change across frequency and field strength, linking conventional electronics, photonics, and extreme-field “petahertz electronics.” The horizontal axis shows increasing electric field strength, from kV m⁻¹ to GV m⁻¹, while the vertical axes compare frequency on the left (MHz to PHz) with corresponding photon energy on the right (neV to eV).\n\nA dashed diagonal boundary and the labels γ < 1, γ = 1, and γ > 1 indicate a transition between field-driven behavior at low frequencies and strong fields, and photon-absorption behavior at higher frequencies. In the lower-left region, gigahertz electronics is depicted as a chip-like device, representing conventional low-frequency electronic control. In the upper-left region, a sinusoidal electric field E(t) represents optics and photonics, where the response is mainly described in terms of absorbing photons.\n\nToward the upper-right, the diagram highlights petahertz electronics as the regime combining very high frequencies with very strong fields. Band-structure sketches labeled CB and VB suggest electrons being driven between valence and conduction bands. A stylized nanoscale device under a strong optical waveform and ammeter implies ultrafast current generation or charge transfer induced directly by the electric field.",
